# Application Security

## Scope

Applies to:
- UI components
- API endpoints
- server-side rendering
- data processing

## 1. XSS Prevention

Never trust or directly render user-provided HTML.

Rules:
- escape user-generated content
- never use `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` unless strictly necessary
- if HTML rendering is required, sanitize using a trusted sanitizer already approved by the project

## 2. CSRF Protection

Mutating endpoints must enforce CSRF protection when cookie-based authentication is involved.

Acceptable strategies include:
- same-site cookies
- CSRF tokens
- double-submit cookie pattern

If cookies are used for auth, CSRF protection is mandatory.

## 3. SSRF Prevention

Server code must never allow arbitrary outbound requests based on untrusted input.

Rules:
- do not allow arbitrary URLs from user input
- validate allowed domains explicitly
- block private, loopback, and link-local network ranges

Disallowed destinations include:
- localhost
- 127.0.0.1
- 169.254.x.x
- 10.x.x.x
- 192.168.x.x
- 172.16.x.x through 172.31.x.x

## 4. Open Redirect Prevention

Never redirect users to URLs provided directly by untrusted input.

Redirects must be validated against:
- internal routes
- explicit trusted allowlists

## 5. Input Normalization

All user inputs must be normalized before use when relevant.

Examples:
- trim whitespace
- normalize casing
- enforce expected formats
- normalize emails where appropriate

## 6. HTML and Markdown Rendering

If rich content is supported:
- sanitize HTML
- restrict allowed tags
- strip script tags
- disable inline event handlers
- never render unsanitized Markdown or HTML into the UI

## 7. Unsafe URL and Redirect Construction

Do not build redirect or navigation targets directly from untrusted input.

Rules:
- validate destination against internal routes or explicit allowlists
- reject protocol-relative or unexpected absolute URLs

## 8. Outbound Request Allowlisting

Any server-side outbound request based on dynamic input must use an explicit allowlist.

Rules:
- validate protocol
- validate hostname
- block private and link-local ranges
- do not proxy arbitrary user-provided URLs

## 9. Safe Rendering Defaults

Treat all untrusted input as hostile by default.

Rules:
- never trust client-supplied role flags, HTML, URLs, or formatting
- keep security-critical decisions on the server
- fail closed when validation or sanitization cannot be guaranteed